This is a known issue when targeting Android 15 (API 35) / .NET 10. Android now enforces **edge-to-edge** rendering, which makes `AdjustResize` behave differently — the system no longer shrinks your window for the keyboard.

**Two options:**

### Option 1 — Opt out of edge-to-edge (quick fix)

In `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
using AndroidX.Core.View;

protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);
    // Tell Android your app handles its own system bar layout
    WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window!, true);
}
```

Keep `WindowSoftInputMode = SoftInput.AdjustResize` on the `[Activity]` attribute. This restores the .NET 9 behavior.

### Option 2 — Handle keyboard insets properly (recommended)

Leave edge-to-edge enabled and react to the keyboard inset in your layout:

```csharp
// In MainActivity.cs or a custom handler
ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window!.DecorView, new KeyboardInsetsListener());

class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        // imeInsets.Bottom is the keyboard height in px
        v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, imeInsets.Bottom);
        return insets;
    }
}
```

This adjusts the bottom padding dynamically so your input bar slides up with the keyboard.

**Option 1** is the fastest path if you just need it working. **Option 2** is forward-compatible with Android's direction.
